{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Moses alone shall draw near to YHWH</b> This too is according to the opinion of R. Abraham Ibn Ezra (on Exodus 24:2), that the name [Moses] stands in place of the pronoun, for it should have said after \"Ascend to YHWH\" — \"and you shall draw near alone,\" but the usage of the language is thus, as I have already mentioned (in the preceding verse). Similarly, \"And YHWH rained upon Sodom and upon Gomorrah brimstone and fire from from YHWH\" (Genesis 19:24) — the meaning is \"from Him.\" Similarly, \"And Moses said to Hobab the son of Reuel the Midianite, Moses' father-in-law\" (Numbers 10:29) — the meaning is \"his father-in-law.\" Similarly, \"And YHWH sent Jerubbaal and Bedan and Jephthah and Samuel\" (I Samuel 12:11). Similarly, \"Then Solomon assembled the elders of Israel and all the heads of the tribes to King Solomon\" (I Kings 8:1).\n\nBut the correct interpretation in my eyes here is that this statement was also directed to Aaron, for he too heard when the voice of YHWH said to Moses: \"Ascend to YHWH — you and Aaron, Nadab and Abihu, and seventy of the elders of Israel, and you shall prostrate yourselves from afar\" (in the preceding verse). If so, it would have been necessary to mention the one drawing near by name, and if He were to speak to him directly, it would likewise have been necessary to specify \"and you, Moses, shall draw near to YHWH,\" and the pronoun alone would not suffice. Therefore it says \"and Moses alone shall draw near, and they shall not draw near,\" and this is the meaning of \"alone\" — to exclude even Aaron, who was included together with him in the statement. And it is possible that this was an allusion to the exalted communication that He would command to be spoken to Moses, that he should draw near to YHWH. And the enlightened will understand:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "In the Genesis 19:24 citation, Ramban writes מֵאֵת ה׳ rendered literally as \"from from YHWH\" to preserve his grammatical point about the locative/possessive use of אֵת; translated as \"from Him\" per his own gloss."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:15:34.257033295Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}